A night of sleep cycles through several stages, repeating roughly every 90 minutes, about 4–6 times per night. The mix shifts as the night goes on: more deep sleep early, more REM later.

```steps
{"items":[
{"title":"Stage N1 (light sleep)","text":"The transition from wakefulness, lasting a few minutes. Muscles relax, brain waves slow, and you can be easily woken.","tag":"light"},
{"title":"Stage N2 (light sleep)","text":"About half the night. Heart rate and temperature drop; bursts of brain activity called sleep spindles and K-complexes appear.","tag":"light"},
{"title":"Stage N3 (deep sleep / slow-wave sleep)","text":"Body repairs itself: tissue growth, immune function, energy restoration. Hardest to wake from; dominant in the first half of the night.","tag":"deep"},
{"title":"REM sleep","text":"Dreams mostly occur here. The brain is active but muscles are temporarily paralyzed. First REM period is short (~10 min), and REM periods lengthen toward morning.","tag":"REM"}
]}
```

Over the night, N3 shrinks and REM stretches out, which is why you're most likely to wake from a vivid dream in the early morning. All stages matter — deep sleep for physical recovery, REM for memory and mood.
